    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A merchant city-state of the Russian north that elected its officials and archbishop and hired princes by contract.</li><li>It ruled a huge fur-producing hinterland to the White Sea and the Urals and traded with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moscow">Moscow</a> crushed its army in 1471, annexed it in 1478 and took away its veche bell.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Archaeologists have found over a thousand birchbark letters in Novgorod, including a thirteenth-century boy Onfim’s school exercises and drawings.</p></aside>
    </section>

